    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Aboriginal peoples had lived around Port Jackson for at least 30,000 years before the First Fleet landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a> in 1788 and founded a penal colony; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1789 killed about half the Aboriginal population of the region.</li><li>The convict town became a city in 1842, grew on the wealth of the gold rushes of the 1850s, and became the capital of the state of New South Wales when the colonies federated in 1901.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> mass migration changed the city, and by 2021 over 40 per cent of its people had been born overseas.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first Aboriginal people to live continuously in the new settlement were survivors of the Sydney clans, who came in under Bennelong in November 1790, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> had killed about half the Aboriginal population of the region.</p></aside>
    </section>

